        "description": "<div id=\"link_wrapped_content\">\n<body><section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>ChatGPT and other big AI chatbots aren\u2019t ones for holding anything back. If you ask them a simple question, you\u2019ll frequently get multi-part answers, complete with bullets and emojis.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Those giant AI-generated answers can be overwhelming. If your intent is to actually learn something rather than being on the receiving end of a term paper, ChatGPT\u2019s lengthy replies might be little more than a waste of tokens.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>For example, when I asked ChatGPT (the \u201cinstant\u201d version) to teach me something about neural networks, a key AI concept that I\u2019m still wrapping my head around, it spat out an 800-word answer in <em>15 parts</em>\u2013no kidding. I didn\u2019t get far before my eyes started to glaze over.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Now, I\u2019ve already written about an AI prompt that helps you learn 80 percent of a concept by distilling the top 20 percent of what you need to know a",
